::ALG2_U02_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertex of f(x) = (x + 1)<sup>2</sup> - 6? {
=(-1, -6) #Correct. x + 1 means h = -1.
~(1, -6) #Sign is reversed.
~(-6, -1) #Coordinates reversed.
~(1, 6) #Both signs are wrong.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the axis of symmetry for f(x) = (x - 7)<sup>2</sup> + 2? {
~x = 2 #This uses the vertical shift k instead of the axis value.
=x = 7 #Correct. x = 7.
~y = 7 #Axis is vertical.
~x = -7 #Sign is reversed.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the minimum value of f(x) = 3(x - 2)<sup>2</sup> - 5? {
~2 #This is x-coordinate.
~3 #This is leading factor.
=-5 #Correct. Minimum output is -5.
~5 #Sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the maximum value of f(x) = -2(x + 4)<sup>2</sup> + 9? {
~-4 #This is x-coordinate.
~-2 #This is leading factor.
~4 #Sign is reversed.
=9 #Correct. Maximum output is 9.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the y-intercept of f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> - 3x - 10? {
=-10 #Correct. f(0) = -10.
~-3 #This is x coefficient.
~10 #Sign is wrong.
~1 #This is leading coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What are the x-intercepts of f(x) = (x + 5)(x - 1)? {
~(5, 0) and (-1, 0) #Signs are reversed.
=(-5, 0) and (1, 0) #Correct. Zeros are -5 and 1.
~(0, -5) and (0, 1) #These are not x-intercepts.
~(-5, 1) and (0, 0) #This mixes coordinates.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x - 3)<sup>2</sup> + 4, what is the range? {
~y &le; 4 #This would open downward.
~x &ge; 3 #Range uses y-values.
=y &ge; 4 #Correct. Minimum output is 4.
~all real numbers #Output is restricted.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = -1(x + 2)<sup>2</sup> + 7, what is the range? {
~y &ge; 7 #It opens downward.
~x &le; -2 #Range uses output values.
~all real numbers #Output is restricted.
=y &le; 7 #Correct. Maximum output is 7.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>A projectile model has zeros t = 0 and t = 6. What does t = 6 usually represent? {
=landing time #Correct. Later zero is landing.
~initial height #Initial time is usually t = 0.
~maximum height #Maximum is at vertex.
~launch time #Launch is t = 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the axis of symmetry for zeros x = -2 and x = 8? {
~x = 6 #Not the midpoint.
=x = 3 #Correct. Average is 3.
~x = -10 #This subtracts incorrectly.
~x = 10 #This adds absolute values.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature gives the highest or lowest point of a parabola? {
~y-intercept #This is where x = 0.
~x-intercept #This is where y = 0.
=vertex #Correct. Vertex is turning point.
~domain #Domain is input set.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>For h(t) = -16t<sup>2</sup> + 64t, when is the object on the ground? {
~t = 2 only #This is maximum time.
~t = 4 only #This is only landing.
~t = 0 only #This is only launch.
=t = 0 and t = 4 #Correct. Factor as -16t(t - 4).
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the vertex of f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 12? {
=(4, -4) #Correct. Complete the square.
~(-4, -4) #Sign is incorrect.
~(4, 12) #This is not vertex.
~(0, 12) #This is y-intercept.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which interval describes where f(x) = (x - 1)<sup>2</sup> decreases? {
~x &gt; 1 #It increases there.
=x &lt; 1 #Correct. It decreases before vertex.
~x = 1 #This is only vertex line.
~all x #It does not decrease everywhere.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which interval describes where f(x) = (x - 1)<sup>2</sup> increases? {
~x &lt; 1 #It decreases there.
~x = 0 #This is one input.
=x &gt; 1 #Correct. It increases after vertex.
~x &le; 1 #Includes decreasing side.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>A ball reaches maximum height at t = 3 seconds. What key feature gives this time? {
~x-intercept #This gives ground time.
~y-intercept #This gives initial height.
~domain #Domain gives input interval.
=vertex #Correct. Vertex gives maximum.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does the y-intercept represent in a height model h(t)? {
=initial height #Correct. h(0) is starting height.
~landing time #Landing is an x-intercept.
~maximum height #Maximum is at vertex.
~axis of symmetry #Axis gives vertex time.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = 2(x + 3)<sup>2</sup> - 1, what is the axis of symmetry? {
~x = 3 #Sign is reversed.
=x = -3 #Correct. Vertex x-coordinate is -3.
~y = -1 #Axis is vertical.
~x = -1 #This uses k incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which domain is usually meaningful for time in a real-world projectile problem? {
~negative values only #Negative time is not meaningful.
~all real numbers always #Context restricts time.
=t &ge; 0 within the flight interval #Correct. Time begins at launch.
~only t = 0 #Motion includes more than launch.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What are the x-intercepts of f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + x - 12? {
~(4, 0) and (-3, 0) #Signs are reversed.
~(0, -4) and (0, 3) #These are not x-intercepts.
~(-12, 0) and (1, 0) #These are not zeros.
=(-4, 0) and (3, 0) #Correct. Factors are (x + 4)(x - 3).
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the range of f(x) = -3(x - 5)<sup>2</sup> + 11? {
=y &le; 11 #Correct. Maximum is 11.
~y &ge; 11 #This would open upward.
~x &le; 5 #Range uses y-values.
~all real numbers #Output is restricted.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which key feature gives where a quadratic crosses the y-axis? {
~vertex #This is turning point.
=y-intercept #Correct. y-intercept occurs when x = 0.
~axis of symmetry #This is vertical line.
~range #This is output set.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>If a quadratic model has vertex (2, 30) and opens downward, what does 30 represent? {
~time at maximum #The time is 2.
~initial value #Initial value is y-intercept.
=maximum output #Correct. Vertex output is maximum.
~zero #A zero has output 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes f(x) = (x + 6)<sup>2</sup>? {
~Minimum value is 6. #Minimum is 0.
~Axis of symmetry is x = 6. #Axis is x = -6.
~Vertex is (0, 6). #Vertex is not (0, 6).
=Vertex is (-6, 0). #Correct. Vertex is (-6, 0).
}

::ALG2_U02_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of a quadratic function with no stated context? {
=all real numbers #Correct. Polynomial functions are defined for all real x.
~x &ge; 0 #This is contextual.
~x &le; 0 #This is contextual.
~only zeros #Zeros are not the domain.
}



